Begin Exploring Related Columns
Below is a step‑by‑step blueprint of how the requested RFM‑based analysis can be prepared in SQLite.  
First we identify the tables / columns that matter and the conditions we must apply, then we successively build 10 exploratory‑to‑final SQL queries.

──────────────────  
1.  Relevant columns and their potential usage
──────────────────  
orders.order_id            : primary key to bring in monetary values (via order_items)  
orders.customer_id         : link to customers table  
orders.order_status        : filter only 'delivered' orders  
orders.order_purchase_timestamp : transaction date → Recency calculation  

customers.customer_id      : link to orders  
customers.customer_unique_id : REQUIRED customer identifier for the task  

order_items.order_id       : link to orders  
order_items.price          : item sales value  
order_items.freight_value  : shipping value (add to sales if wanted)  

(CONDITION) orders.order_status = 'delivered' – only completed deliveries will be kept.

──────────────────  
2.  How Recency will be calculated
──────────────────  
Recency (R) = julianday(MAX(order_purchase_timestamp_per_dataset))  
              − julianday(MAX(order_purchase_timestamp_per_customer))  

The smaller the Recency value, the more recent the customer’s last purchase.

──────────────────  
3.  RFM scoring / segment criteria (example)
──────────────────  
• Compute R, F, M scores separately by splitting each measure into terciles  
  (High = 3, Medium = 2, Low = 1).  
• Concatenate the scores, e.g. R3F3M3 → ‘Champions’, R1F1M1 → ‘Lost’.  
  (Feel free to refine; the SQL below produces the three‑level 3/2/1 scores.)

──────────────────  
4.  Ten incremental SQL queries
──────────────────  

```sql
-- 1. Inspect all order statuses that exist
SELECT DISTINCT order_status
FROM orders
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Pull a sample of distinct customer_unique_id values
SELECT DISTINCT customer_unique_id
FROM customers
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Count how many orders are actually delivered
SELECT COUNT(*) AS delivered_orders
FROM orders
WHERE order_status = 'delivered';
```

```sql
-- 4. Take a quick look at delivered orders along with purchase time
SELECT order_id,
       order_purchase_timestamp
FROM orders
WHERE order_status = 'delivered'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Monetary value per delivered order (price + freight) – inspection
SELECT oi.order_id,
       ROUND(SUM(oi.price + oi.freight_value),2) AS order_sales
FROM order_items AS oi
JOIN orders       AS o  USING (order_id)
WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
GROUP BY oi.order_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Build a view with one row per delivered order (order‑level monetary value)
CREATE TEMP VIEW v_order_sales AS
SELECT oi.order_id,
       o.order_purchase_timestamp,
       o.customer_id,
       ROUND(SUM(oi.price + oi.freight_value),2) AS order_sales
FROM   order_items AS oi
JOIN   orders      AS o  USING (order_id)
WHERE  o.order_status = 'delivered'
GROUP  BY oi.order_id;
```

```sql
-- 7. Join the view to customers and roll up to customer_unique_id level
CREATE TEMP VIEW v_customer_rfm AS
SELECT  c.customer_unique_id,
        COUNT(*)                       AS frequency,          -- F
        ROUND(SUM(v.order_sales),2)    AS monetary,           -- M
        MAX(v.order_purchase_timestamp) AS last_purchase_ts   -- needed for Recency
FROM     v_order_sales AS v
JOIN     customers     AS c  ON c.customer_id = v.customer_id
GROUP BY c.customer_unique_id;
```

```sql
-- 8. Add Recency (days since last purchase) and 3‑quantile scoring
WITH rfm_raw AS (
    SELECT *,
           CAST(julianday((SELECT MAX(order_purchase_timestamp) FROM v_order_sales))
                - julianday(last_purchase_ts) AS INTEGER) AS recency_days
    FROM v_customer_rfm
),
quantiles AS (
    SELECT
        percentile_cont(0.33) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY recency_days) AS r33,
        percentile_cont(0.66) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY recency_days) AS r66,
        percentile_cont(0.33) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY frequency    ) AS f33,
        percentile_cont(0.66) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY frequency    ) AS f66,
        percentile_cont(0.33) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY monetary     ) AS m33,
        percentile_cont(0.66) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY monetary     ) AS m66
    FROM rfm_raw
)
SELECT r.customer_unique_id,
       r.recency_days,
       r.frequency,
       r.monetary,
       CASE
           WHEN r.recency_days <= q.r33 THEN 3
           WHEN r.recency_days <= q.r66 THEN 2
           ELSE 1
       END AS r_score,
       CASE
           WHEN r.frequency  >= q.f66 THEN 3
           WHEN r.frequency  >= q.f33 THEN 2
           ELSE 1
       END AS f_score,
       CASE
           WHEN r.monetary   >= q.m66 THEN 3
           WHEN r.monetary   >= q.m33 THEN 2
           ELSE 1
       END AS m_score
FROM   rfm_raw r
CROSS JOIN quantiles q
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Convert R/F/M scores to segment name and compute AVG sales/order
WITH scored AS (
    /* same CTE as query 8, but keeping all rows */
    SELECT r.*,
           CASE
               WHEN r.recency_days <= q.r33 THEN 3
               WHEN r.recency_days <= q.r66 THEN 2
               ELSE 1
           END AS r_score,
           CASE
               WHEN r.frequency  >= q.f66 THEN 3
               WHEN r.frequency  >= q.f33 THEN 2
               ELSE 1
           END AS f_score,
           CASE
               WHEN r.monetary   >= q.m66 THEN 3
               WHEN r.monetary   >= q.m33 THEN 2
               ELSE 1
           END AS m_score
    FROM rfm_raw r
    CROSS JOIN quantiles q
),
labeled AS (
    SELECT *,
           CASE
               WHEN r_score = 3 AND f_score = 3 AND m_score = 3 THEN 'Champions'
               WHEN r_score = 3 AND f_score = 2 AND m_score = 2 THEN 'Loyal'
               WHEN r_score = 2 AND f_score = 3 AND m_score = 3 THEN 'Potential Loyalist'
               WHEN r_score = 1 AND f_score = 1 AND m_score = 1 THEN 'Lost'
               ELSE 'Others'
           END AS rfm_segment,
           ROUND(monetary * 1.0 / frequency,2) AS avg_sales_per_order
    FROM scored
)
SELECT  rfm_segment,
        COUNT(*)                            AS customers,
        ROUND(AVG(avg_sales_per_order),2)   AS avg_sales_segment
FROM    labeled
GROUP BY rfm_segment;
```

```sql
-- 10. Inspect differences in average sales across segments (full list)
SELECT  rfm_segment,
        ROUND(AVG(avg_sales_per_order),2) AS avg_sales_per_order,
        ROUND(MIN(avg_sales_per_order),2) AS min_customer_avg,
        ROUND(MAX(avg_sales_per_order),2) AS max_customer_avg,
        COUNT(*)                          AS num_customers
FROM    labeled
GROUP BY rfm_segment
ORDER BY avg_sales_per_order DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

──────────────────  
5.  Reading the final outputs
──────────────────  
• The last two queries (9, 10) provide the required “average sales per order” per customer, aggregated by the RFM segment.  
• avg_sales_segment (query 9) or avg_sales_per_order (query 10) highlights how much, on average, each customer in a given segment spends per order.  
• Comparing these numbers lets you articulate differences, e.g.  
  – Champions normally show the highest average sales/order.  
  – Lost customers spend the least, etc.

You may tweak threshold rules or add more granular segments; the showcased SQL pattern will still apply.
